# The asteroid - a twice-subdivided icosphere displaced by noise, authored at
# radius 1 because the game scales one mesh per rock (RenderShape scale is the
# rock's radius in metres), so every rock in the galaxy is this hull at a
# different size and tumble.
#
# ⚑⚑ THIS PART IS BAKED, AND IT IS THE FIRST ASSET IN THE GAME THAT IS. The rule
# decided at the Phase 9 D checkpoint is that a hand edit bakes THE PARTS IT
# TOUCHED and leaves everything else parametric; this file is that rule's other
# half - geometry the parametric vocabulary cannot honestly describe is written
# out literally rather than having a primitive invented to fit it.
#
# ⚑ AND THE REASON IS NOT THAT THE RECIPE IS COMPLICATED. It is that the recipe
# is WRONG in a way that has to be preserved: the displacement is keyed on a
# hash the generator script wrote as FNV-1a and PowerShell did not compute as
# FNV-1a, because `[uint32] * 16777619` promotes to double and rounds to 53 bits
# before the mask takes it back to 32. Every rock the player has ever flown past
# is built on that. A `noise_sphere` primitive reproducing it would put a
# bug-compatibility shim in the engine's permanent vocabulary, inherited by
# every future asset built with it, to serve exactly one caller.
#
# The recipe itself is not lost. It lives in engine/test/geometry/
# shipped_meshes.hpp as buildAsteroid(), pinned to the same hashes this file is,
# which is what makes the two one asset with a regression net rather than two
# implementations. Regenerating a DIFFERENT rock means running that and baking
# the result - deliberately a conscious act, since it re-authors a shipped asset.
#
# ⚑ Its measured radius is 1.1584 m and models.toml authors 1.0, so the sim's
# collision sphere sits inside the hull. Recorded, not corrected: which number
# moves is a decision about how close a ship may come to a rock.
name = "asteroid"

[[part]]
id = "hull"
type = "mesh"
